## Phase 1 — Close the Validation Gap (the 0,0 bug)

**Branch:** `step-1-validation-fixes`

**Objective:** Fix the confirmed backend bug that lets stops save with null/zero coordinates silently, then trace and fix the Android-side cause.

**Deliverables:**
- Add `@Valid` to `CreateTripRequest.stops` so nested `TripStopRequest` validation actually runs
- Add a custom check rejecting `0.0, 0.0` explicitly (not just null) — a real address is never going to be off the coast of Africa
- Trace the Android submission flow for where the selected suggestion's lat/lng gets lost (state binding vs. async race vs. field-name mismatch)
- Add a request/response log (temporary, dev-only) on `/api/trips` and `/api/trips/{id}/stops` to confirm the actual payload being sent

**Kiro Features:**
| Feature | How it's used |
|---------|--------------|
| Spec session | "Add cascading validation to CreateTripRequest.stops and reject 0,0 coordinates explicitly" |
| Supervised mode | Review the validation annotation changes hunk-by-hunk — this is a correctness-critical fix, not boilerplate |
| Hooks | `PostFileSave` on `*.java` → run a quick compile/test check so the fix doesn't silently break other request paths |

**Other Tools:**
| Tool | Usage |
|------|-------|
| Postman / curl | Reproduce the bug with a crafted request, confirm the 400 after the fix |
| Git | Commit the validation fix separately from the request-logging (temporary/dev-only) change |

**What to check first:**
- [ ] Reproduce the bug locally: pick a suggestion in the app, submit, and log the exact outgoing JSON before it hits the backend
- [ ] Confirm whether the JSON has `latitude`/`longitude` present and non-zero, or missing/zero at the point it leaves the client
- [ ] If present but backend still stores 0,0 — bug is in `TripServiceImpl`/`TripStopServiceImpl`; if missing/zero on the client — bug is in the Android suggestion-picker state

> These three are **blocked, not done**. They require running the Android client, which lives
> in a separate repository. The backend-side instrumentation they depend on is in place: enable
> `logging.level.com.example1.getyourride.service.impl=DEBUG` (commented line in
> `application.properties`) and the received lat/lng is logged before persistence on all four
> stop-writing paths. Whoever runs the client next can complete these without further backend work.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [x] Submitting a stop/trip with missing or zero coordinates returns a `400` with a clear validation message, never a silent save
- [ ] Creating a trip via a real suggestion pick results in stored coordinates matching the suggestion, verified by direct DB query
- [x] `pytest`/equivalent backend test added covering the null-stop-in-list case that previously slipped through

**Status:** ⚠️ Backend complete, one criterion unverifiable in this environment.

**Notes on deviations from the original plan:**
- The 0,0 check is implemented as a reusable class-level Bean Validation constraint
  (`validation/ValidCoordinates` + `CoordinatesValidator`) rather than inline field annotations,
  because "is this pair of numbers a real place" is a property of the lat/lng combination and the
  same rule has to hold on four separate write paths.
- Scope was widened by one file beyond the written deliverables: `BookCarpoolRequest` also lacked
  `@Valid` on its nested stops, so `POST /api/trips/{id}/book` was a second route to the same
  silent 0,0 save. Fixing only `CreateTripRequest` would have left the bug reachable.
- `GlobalExceptionHandler` now also reports non-field (global) validation errors. It previously
  read only `getFieldErrors()`, so a class-level constraint violation would have produced a `400`
  with an empty body — the same class of silent failure this phase exists to remove.
- The second acceptance criterion cannot be verified here: it needs a reachable database, and
  neither `localhost` MySQL nor the documented TiDB Cloud instance is available from this
  environment. Manual steps to verify it are in the Phase 1 section of `doc/ARCHITECTURE.md`.
- Tests are plain Bean Validation / `SpringValidatorAdapter` unit tests, not `@SpringBootTest`.
  The existing `@SpringBootTest` classes (`TripControllerTest`, `GetYourRideApplicationTests`)
  already fail without a live MySQL — 12 errors, unchanged by this phase — so a Spring-context
  test would not have run. 27 new tests, all passing, no DB required.

