## Phase 3 — WebSocket / STOMP Infrastructure

**Branch:** `step-4-websocket`

**Objective:** Add the real-time transport layer that doesn't exist yet in the repo.

**Deliverables:**
- `WebSocketConfig` registering a STOMP endpoint (e.g. `/ws`) with a simple broker on `/topic`
- `LocationUpdateDTO` and `StopEventDTO` message classes
- A test publisher (manually triggered) to confirm messages reach a subscribed client before the scheduler exists

**What to check first:**
- [x] Confirm Spring Boot has `spring-boot-starter-websocket` as a dependency (check `pom.xml`/`build.gradle`)
- [ ] Test the endpoint with `wscat` or Postman's WebSocket support before writing any Android client code

> It was **not** a dependency — added in this phase, version managed by `spring-boot-starter-parent`.
> The `wscat` check is blocked: it needs a running instance, which needs a reachable database.
> Confirmed `spring-security-messaging` is *not* on the classpath, so there is no STOMP CSRF
> interception to configure.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] A client can subscribe to `/topic/trip/{tripId}` and receive a manually-published test message
- [x] Message shapes match the documented JSON in `GetYourRide_Tracking_Documentation.md` §4.4

**Status:** ⚠️ Code complete and unit-tested. Message contract verified; end-to-end subscribe test needs a running instance.

**Notes on deviations from the original plan:**
- The test publisher is **disabled by default**, behind
  `getyourride.tracking.test-publisher.enabled=true` (commented line in `application.properties`).
  While enabled, any authenticated caller can broadcast a fabricated vehicle position to a trip's
  subscribers, so it must not ship on. With the flag absent the bean is not registered and the routes
  return 404. Enabling it is required to complete the first acceptance criterion.
- Added `TrackingBroadcastService` beyond the listed deliverables. It is the seam Phase 4's simulator
  publishes through, so the destination format and message construction live in one place and the
  simulator stays unit-testable without a broker.
- Added `TrackingMessageType` and `StopEventStatus` enums plus a `TrackingMessage` interface. Enums
  because the wire values are a closed set and `doc/project-rules.md` prefers enums for new
  status-like fields; the interface so the broadcast service has one payload type and any future
  message shape is forced to carry the `type` discriminator.
- Messages live in a new `dto/message` package and keep the `...DTO` suffix from the deliverables
  rather than the `XxxRequest`/`XxxResponse` convention, because they are neither — they are
  server-pushed messages.
- `lat`/`lng` field names kept as documented even though the rest of the codebase uses
  `latitude`/`longitude`. The contract wins; renaming would break the Android client silently.
- No SockJS and no application destination prefix, both deliberate and both pinned by tests. See the
  Live Tracking Transport section of `doc/ARCHITECTURE.md` for the reasoning.
- 18 new tests (`TrackingMessageContractTest`, `TrackingBroadcastServiceImplTest`,
  `WebSocketConfigTest`), all passing, no broker or database required.

> ⚠️ **Security gap carried into Phase 5:** the `/ws` handshake is authenticated (it is not in the
> public matcher list, so `JwtAuthFilter` applies), but STOMP *subscriptions* are not authorised
> per-trip. Any authenticated user can subscribe to `/topic/trip/{tripId}` and watch any trip's live
> position. Closing it needs a `ChannelInterceptor` on the inbound channel checking SUBSCRIBE frames,
> plus a policy decision on who may watch a trip — the driver, students with a `trip_stop` on it,
> students with a `trip_booking`? Note `trip_booking` has no entity mapping, so booking-based access
> is not currently queryable. Not guessed at here.

