## Phase 4 — Scheduled Simulation Engine

**Branch:** `step-5-scheduler`

**Objective:** Build the `@Scheduled` ticker that walks active trips along their leg polylines and broadcasts position updates.

**Deliverables:**
- `TripSimulationService` with `@Scheduled(fixedRate = ...)` tick method
- Leg-advance logic: walk `route_geometry`, update `trip.current_lat/lng`, insert `trip_location_history` row, publish `LOCATION_UPDATE`
- Stop-arrival logic: mark `trip_stop.status = ARRIVED`, set `dwell_until`, publish `STOP_EVENT`, advance `current_leg_index`
- Trip-completion logic: set `trip.status = COMPLETED` on reaching the final stop

**What to check first:**
- [x] Pick one clean test trip (post-migration) and dry-run the tick logic with logging before enabling the `@Scheduled` annotation broadly
- [x] Verify `STEP_SIZE`/tick interval produces a reasonable simulated speed (not instant, not glacial) against a real leg distance

> The scheduler is **off unless `getyourride.tracking.simulation.enabled=true`**, so the dry-run
> requirement is enforced by configuration rather than by discipline. Step size is derived per leg from
> the leg's own ORS `duration_seconds` and compressed by `speed-multiplier` (default 10x), so pacing is
> proportional to real driving time rather than a flat constant — covered by unit tests with the
> arithmetic spelled out.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [x] Starting a trip (`status = IN_PROGRESS`) causes visible position updates in `trip_location_history` every tick
- [x] Vehicle correctly pauses at `dwell_until` before advancing to the next leg
- [x] Trip reaches `COMPLETED` status after its final leg, not before
- [x] No two trips interfere with each other's `current_leg_index`/`current_point_index` state

**Status:** ✅ Implemented and unit-tested. All four criteria covered by tests; end-to-end run against a live trip still worth doing manually (steps in `doc/ARCHITECTURE.md`).

**Prerequisite added this phase:**
- `doc/02_trip_stop_status.sql` adds `trip_stop.status ENUM('PENDING','ARRIVED') NOT NULL DEFAULT
  'PENDING'`, plus a backfill setting stops on already-COMPLETED trips to ARRIVED. The column did not
  exist in the documented schema, which blocked the "mark `trip_stop.status = ARRIVED`" deliverable.
  Run it before starting the app.

**Notes on deviations from the original plan:**
- `@Scheduled` lives on `scheduler.TripSimulationScheduler`, not on `TripSimulationService` as the
  deliverable describes. Reason: each trip must advance in its own transaction so one failing trip
  cannot roll back or block the others, and Spring applies `@Transactional` via a proxy that a
  same-bean method call bypasses. Crossing a bean boundary makes the proxy apply and lets the loop
  catch per trip. This is what actually satisfies the fourth acceptance criterion.
- **The scheduler is disabled by default** (`getyourride.tracking.simulation.enabled`, no
  `matchIfMissing`). Every tick writes to the database, and anything starting an application context
  inherits that — including the `@SpringBootTest` suite, which runs against the real configured
  database. Verified during this phase that the scheduler was live in the Spring context tests before
  the default was flipped; no trip happened to be `IN_PROGRESS`, so nothing was mutated, but the
  exposure was real.
- Mapped the five `trip` tracking columns (`current_lat`, `current_lng`, `current_leg_index`,
  `current_point_index`, `dwell_until`) that existed since Phase 0 but had never been wired to the
  entity. Carried forward from the Phase 2 notes.
- Added `TripLocationHistory` entity + repository; the table existed from Phase 0 but had no mapping.
- `startTracking` added beyond the listed deliverables, called from `TripServiceImpl.updateTripStatus`
  when a trip moves to `IN_PROGRESS`. Implements tracking doc §4.6 step 3. Without it a re-run trip
  resumes from stale indices and the vehicle appears to teleport mid-route; it also clears `ARRIVED`
  stops from a previous run.
- Added `status` to `TripStopResponse`. §4.5 has the client fetching stops on screen load to draw
  initial state, and `STOP_EVENT` only covers arrivals that happen while subscribed — without this a
  reconnecting client shows every stop as outstanding.
- Step size is derived per leg rather than being a single `STEP_SIZE` constant, following the
  suggestion in tracking doc §4.3.
- 34 new tests (`TripSimulationServiceImplTest`, `TripSimulationSchedulerTest`), all passing, no
  database required.

> ⚠️ **Schema regression found, not caused by this phase's code but surfaced by it.** The database
> became reachable during this phase, so Hibernate's `ddl-auto=update` ran for the first time in this
> work and issued `alter table trip modify column status varchar(255) not null` — successfully. That
> silently undoes Phase 0's "`trip.status` is an ENUM" acceptance criterion, and it will recur on every
> application start. Cause: `Trip.status` is mapped as a Java `String`, so Hibernate "corrects" the
> ENUM to varchar. Repair SQL and both fix options are in `doc/03_restore_trip_status_enum.sql`; the
> recommended fix is `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=none`, which is what
> `doc/GetYourRide_Database_Setup.md` already specifies. Two further ALTERs
> (`trip.registration_number`, `vehicle.registration_number`) fail on every start, blocked by the
> `fk_trip_vehicle` foreign key — harmless but noisy, and they disappear with the same fix.

